From: postmarketOS <bramble@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] net: qrtr: accept new-server messages with zero node and port from local sockets

libqrtr's qrtr_new_server() sends QRTR_TYPE_NEW_SERVER with the node and
port fields at zero and leaves it to the name service to use the sender's
address. The mainline kernel name service does that. This kernel checks the
message in qrtr_sendmsg() first: it looks up the server node, and node 0 is
the modem here, so the message is "not forwardable" and is dropped without
an error (sendto() returns 0).

Programs that publish a service this way never show up in qrtr-lookup. This
stopped 81voltd, the IMS Data service that the modem needs before it brings
up VoLTE, from registering. Fill in the sender's node and port when both are
zero and the message goes to the local name service.

---
--- a/net/qrtr/qrtr.c
+++ b/net/qrtr/qrtr.c
@@ -1741,8 +1741,22 @@
 	if (addr->sq_port == QRTR_PORT_CTRL && type == QRTR_TYPE_NEW_SERVER) {
 		ipc->state = QRTR_STATE_MULTI;
 
+		skb_copy_bits(skb, 0, &pkt, sizeof(pkt));
+
+		/* libqrtr (qrtr_new_server) leaves the node and port of a
+		 * local server at zero and relies on the name service to
+		 * fill them in, as the mainline kernel name service does.
+		 * Zero is the modem's node here, so the check below would
+		 * drop the message. Describe the sender instead.
+		 */
+		if (addr->sq_node == ipc->us.sq_node &&
+		    !pkt.server.node && !pkt.server.port) {
+			pkt.server.node = cpu_to_le32(ipc->us.sq_node);
+			pkt.server.port = cpu_to_le32(ipc->us.sq_port);
+			skb_store_bits(skb, 0, &pkt, sizeof(pkt));
+		}
+
 		/* drop new server cmds that are not forwardable to dst node*/
-		skb_copy_bits(skb, 0, &pkt, sizeof(pkt));
 		srv_node = qrtr_node_lookup(le32_to_cpu(pkt.server.node));
 		if (!qrtr_must_forward(srv_node, node, type)) {
 			rc = 0;
